KW  - Spark plasma sintering
KW  - Ceramics
KW  - Kinetics
KW  - Mechanism
KW  - Models
AB  - A comprehensive overview of spark plasma sintering (SPS) kinetics is provided in this work, motivated by the need to identify rate-controlling mechanisms and improve predictive modeling of ceramics sintering. The main sintering models reported in the literature have been summarized, from classical diffusion-based formulations to advanced computational approaches. Arrhenius-type models treat densification as a thermally activated process, expressing shrinkage rates in terms of an apparent activation energy. The master sintering curve (MSC) approach extends classical models by correlating densification with a time-temperature integral, assuming a single dominant mechanism. Reported activation energies exhibit substantial variability; for example, values for Al2O3 densification using the Arrhenius model range from approximately 173.6 to 420 kJ/mol, whereas values derived from the MSC range from 290 to 490 kJ/mol. This highlights the strong dependence of apparent Q on material state, sintering stage, processing conditions, and kinetic analysis. This review also highlights how key processing parameters govern SPS kinetics. Temperature as the primary driver can shift the dominant sintering mechanism. For Al2O3, reported activation energies vary substantially with sintering temperature, ranging from approximately 163 to 440 kJ/mol across different studies. These variations are associated with changes in the dominant densification mechanisms at different stages of sintering, highlighting the temperature-dependent nature of the apparent activation energy. Heating rate alters the thermal trajectory of densification; moderate rates favor diffusion-controlled densification with minimal grain growth. Applied pressure markedly enhances kinetics by lowering diffusion barriers and promoting particle rearrangement and plastic deformation, enabling faster densification at lower temperatures. Meanwhile, the electric field/current in SPS boosts mass transport and can even modify grain boundary behavior. Additives and second-phase inclusions provide another leverage on kinetics by adjusting the apparent activation energy (Q) and densification rate or inhibiting grain coarsening. Likewise, refining the particle size shortens diffusion distances and increases interface area, generally accelerating densification. By correlating theoretical predictions with reported kinetic data across many ceramic systems, the operative mechanisms under various SPS conditions can be mapped, revealing consistent trends and outliers.
