AB  - The rapid growth of digital entertainment media has intensified the complexity of online news dissemination, where abnormal propagation patterns and polarized audience emotions may influence user engagement, public perception, and the credibility of entertainment-related content. To address this issue, this study proposes a semi-supervised deep learning framework for anomaly detection and sentiment analysis in entertainment news dissemination. First, the dissemination process is represented as a propagation graph, in which structural relationships among news items, interaction behaviors, and diffusion paths are modeled through a graph neural network. By learning the graph-based characteristics of entertainment news circulation, the proposed method identifies potential abnormal patterns embedded in the dissemination process. To reduce dependence on manually labeled samples, a semi-supervised learning strategy is introduced, combining pseudo-label generation and contrastive learning to enhance the model's ability to exploit unlabeled data. For sentiment analysis, a deep neural feature extraction module is designed to capture textual sentiment cues, while multimodal information is further integrated to improve the robustness of audience emotion recognition. In addition, semantic relation modeling is incorporated to capture fine-grained emotional expressions, particularly in complex linguistic contexts such as metaphor and pun. Experimental results show that the proposed method achieves an accuracy of 61.0% in standard news category recognition and a recall of 48.56% in anomaly detection, indicating its potential for identifying irregular dissemination behaviors while also revealing room for further improvement. In sentiment analysis, the model achieves 71.0% accuracy for positive emotion recognition and 34.1% accuracy for negative emotion recognition. After optimization, the average training time per round is reduced from 3.80 h to 3.67 h. These findings suggest that semi-supervised graph-based deep learning can provide a feasible computational approach for monitoring entertainment news dissemination and understanding audience sentiment in digital media environments.
