AB  - Aqueous Zn-ion batteries (AZIBs) have emerged as attractive energy-storage systems owing to the natural abundance of zinc, high intrinsic safety, fast ionic conductivity of aqueous electrolytes, and low manufacturing cost. Nevertheless, their electrochemical performance is strongly governed by the rational design of both cathode and anode materials. Among various candidates, molybdenum disulfide (MoS₂) stands out due to its unique layered structure and tunable phase chemistry. In particular, phase-transformed 1 T/2H MoS₂ offers a powerful platform for interlayer modulation, enabling enlarged interlayer spacing, enhanced electronic conductivity, and optimized Zn2+ diffusion kinetics. The coexistence of metallic 1 T and semiconducting 2H phases creates abundant active sites and facilitates reversible Zn2+ (de)intercalation, effectively addressing issues such as sluggish ion transport, structural instability, and cathode dissolution. This review highlights the novelty of employing phase-engineered MoS₂ cathodes in AZIBs, emphasizing how interlayer engineering and phase synergy significantly improve rate capability and cycling stability. Equally important, it discusses compatible zinc anode optimization strategies to mitigate dendrite growth, hydrogen evolution, and corrosion, which remain critical bottlenecks. By integrating advanced 1 T/2H MoS₂ cathode design with tactically engineered Zn anodes, this work underscores a synergistic approach that enhances overall battery efficiency and durability. Furthermore, the mechanical flexibility of such systems is explored, demonstrating their potential for next-generation wearable and flexible energy-storage applications.
