AB  - Against the backdrop of Vietnam’s ambitious policy to primarily permit electric vehicles (EVs) in its two largest cities by 2030, understanding public discourse is critical. This study quantitatively investigates the evolving public discourse by analyzing over 42,000 comments from 1,173 VnExpress articles between 2022 and 2024. Utilizing inductive content analysis and Chi-squared testing, we reveal a significant maturation in the conversation: discussions on systemic infrastructure challenges more than doubled from 9% to 21% of the total discourse, becoming a dominant concern. Crucially, we quantify a powerful “VinFast effect,” where articles focused on the national brand exhibited 79% positive sentiment in 2024 compared to just 34% for non-VinFast articles. This “sentiment halo” significantly mitigates concerns over cost and performance but fails to alleviate anxiety regarding infrastructure readiness. The study contributes a nuanced model of technology diffusion, extending Diffusion of Innovations theory by introducing a national-identity dimension where a domestic champion accelerates acceptance but cannot resolve state-level structural barriers.
