AB  - Essential oil nanoemulsions (EONEs) are commonly prepared using high-energy techniques such as ultrasonic emulsification, producing small droplets with a large interfacial area. However, reduced droplet size increases Laplace pressure and the chemical potential of dispersed oil molecules, making EONEs prone to Ostwald ripening (OR). OR is a thermodynamically driven destabilization process in which molecular diffusion from smaller to larger droplets leads to progressive droplet growth. This review summarizes key strategies to control OR and their impact on antimicrobial performance: (1) carrier or co-oils that reduce oil solubility and slow OR but may dilute bioactive EO constituents and reduce biological activity; (2) lipid-based co-surfactants that enhance interfacial barrier properties, limit molecular diffusion, and generally preserve antimicrobial efficacy; and (3) selection of EO constituents with lower aqueous solubility, which improves stability without necessarily compromising activity. Beyond antimicrobial activity, OR-induced droplet growth impairs bioactive delivery by reducing the retention of active compounds within droplets, altering controlled release kinetics, diminishing bioaccessibility at the target interface, and potentially limiting bioavailability; the formulation strategies discussed herein are therefore relevant to any application where EO concentration at the site of action determines functional outcome. Comparative evidence from four well-known antimicrobial compounds, including thymol, carvacrol, eugenol, and cinnamaldehyde, indicates that physicochemical properties of the compound, especially aqueous solubility and hydrophobicity, significantly affect both stability and antimicrobial efficacy of EONEs. High aqueous solubility or low hydrophobicity increases diffusion-driven OR, whereas more hydrophobic compounds form more stable emulsions and exhibit stronger membrane interactions with microbial cells. Overall, interfacial engineering and compositional design provide complementary strategies to balance stability and antimicrobial efficacy in food-grade EONEs. Finally, practical formulation guidelines for optimizing controlled release and improving bioaccessibility are presented, including balancing carrier oil content to avoid excessive dilution of active compounds, tailoring surfactant systems to strengthen interfacial barriers and reduce diffusional transport, and selecting EO mixtures with lower aqueous solubility while maintaining biological functionality.
