AB  - The integration of conducting polymers (CPs) with emerging boron-based two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials has attracted considerable attention due to their exceptional physicochemical properties and multifunctional application potential. Borophene, boron carbon nitride (BCN), MBenes, and metal borides exhibit remarkable electrical conductivity, tunable electronic structures, abundant active sites, and unique surface chemistry, making them promising candidates for advanced energy, sensing, catalytic, and optoelectronic applications. However, their practical implementation is often restricted by structural instability, agglomeration, and limited processability. The incorporation of CPs such as polyaniline (PANI), polypyrrole (PPy), PEDOT:PSS, polycarbazole (PCz), and poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) effectively improves conductivity, flexibility, charge transfer, electrochemical stability, and interfacial interactions through synergistic effects. This review comprehensively summarizes recent advances in CP/borophene, CP/BCN, and CP/MBene hybrid systems, focusing on synthesis strategies, structural characteristics, interfacial engineering, and multifunctional properties. Particular emphasis is placed on the role of CPs in enhancing electron transport, ion diffusion, dielectric behaviour, catalytic activity, and structural stability. Furthermore, the applications of these hybrid systems in energy storage and conversion, sensing, catalysis, optoelectronics, electromagnetic shielding, and flexible electronic technologies are systematically highlighted. Recent studies demonstrate that CP-assisted hybridization significantly enhances electrochemical and functional performance, yielding high capacitance, excellent cycling stability, low overpotentials, rapid sensing response, and superior electromagnetic attenuation. Finally, current challenges associated with scalable synthesis, long-term stability, interface engineering, and practical device integration are discussed, along with future research opportunities for developing advanced multifunctional boron-based CP nanocomposites for sustainable energy and next-generation electronic technologies.
