KW  - Nanoconfinement effect
KW  - Confined fluid microstructure
KW  - Anomalous diffusion
KW  - Wall slip
KW  - Process intensification
AB  - Nanoconfined fluids exhibit unique transport behaviors that deviate fundamentally from the predictions of macroscopic transport models based on no-slip boundary conditions. The lack of theoretical frameworks has limited the progress toward disruptive separation and reaction nanotechnologies. This review synthesizes recent advances in nanoconfined transport from four perspectives. First, we examine the transport of fluids ranging from small molecules to polymers under nanoconfinement, and summarize their microstructural features, anomalous diffusion, and flow enhancement, as well as their correlations with key factors such as wall wettability, channel dimensions, and external fields. Then, the density, viscosity, slip, and flow models are outlined to better describe nanoconfined transport phenomena. Furthermore, nano- to microscale experimental techniques for probing confined-fluid structure and transport characteristics are surveyed, with emphasis on their spatiotemporal resolution and system compatibility. Finally, the representative applications of confined transport and confinement effects are introduced, such as catalytic reactions, membrane separation, and composite material processing. In this review, the transport properties and theoretical models of fluids in nanoconfined channels are highlighted, which is critical to the rational design and application of nanofluidic devices, such as nanofiltration membranes, nanoreactors and lab-on-a-chip systems. Future research should focus on integrating nanoconfined transport models into macroscopic theoretical frameworks to guide industrial scale-up design, while addressing practical challenges including membrane fouling and catalyst deactivation.
ER  - 

