AB  - The stratification characteristics of mixed components exert a significant influence on the storage, transportation, and separation of gas–liquid systems. Stratification may occur under a gravitational field due to differences in the molar masses of component molecules. However, molecular Brownian motion tends to promote uniform diffusion within the system. By means of experiments, CFD simulations, and MD simulations, this study classifies and summarizes the relative influence and dominance of gravitational and diffusive effects on stratification in homogeneous gas and liquid systems. The factors governing these two effects are also analyzed. Specifically, temperature, pressure, and initial composition primarily affect stratification trends by influencing the extent of molecular diffusion, whereas density, stratification height, and body forces govern stratification through gravitational effects. Furthermore, for this dynamic process, time and phase state play critical roles in determining stratification outcomes. At present, relatively few studies have focused on the non-equilibrium microscopic mechanisms of stratification in gaseous and supercritical states, as well as their potential developmental trends.
