KW  - Interlayer engineering
KW  - Mg diffusion
KW  - Structural water
KW  - Defect engineering
AB  - Magnesium batteries have attracted increasing attention as promising post-lithium energy-storage systems because of the abundance, low cost, and safety potential of magnesium. However, the high charge density and strong polarizing ability of Mg2+ lead to sluggish diffusion kinetics, severe interfacial polarization, and structural instability in conventional cathode materials. Layered vanadium oxides are considered attractive cathode candidates owing to their open V–O frameworks, multivalent vanadium redox chemistry, and highly tunable interlayer regions. Nevertheless, their practical Mg storage performance is still limited by strong Mg2+–host interactions, poor electronic conductivity, and irreversible structural evolution during repeated magnesiation/demagnesiation. Interlayer engineering provides an effective approach to address these issues by regulating the interlayer spacing, guest species, local coordination environment, electronic structure, and defect chemistry. In this review, the structural characteristics and Mg storage challenges of layered vanadium oxide cathodes are first summarized. Representative interlayer engineering strategies, including metal-ion/structural-water co-regulation, organic-molecule or conducting-polymer intercalation, and defect-coupled interlayer modulation, are then discussed with emphasis on their roles in enhancing ionic conductivity, electronic conductivity, and structural stability. The correlations among interlayer chemistry, Mg2+ diffusion behavior, charge-transfer kinetics, and electrochemical performance are further analyzed. Finally, the outstanding challenges and future research directions are discussed, with emphasis on mechanistic clarification, electrolyte compatibility, quantitative interlayer design, and multi-strategy integration toward high-performance magnesium batteries.
