AB  - Steel slag carbonated bricks enable slag valorization and long-term CO2 sequestration by converting reactive calcium and magnesium phases into stable carbonates. This review examines steel slag mineralogy and carbonation kinetics, and analyzes how raw materials, forming, curing, crystal morphology, and product distribution affect mechanical properties; enhancement strategies including pretreatment, hydration-carbonation synergy, and microstructure regulation are summarized. The diffusion-limited nature of carbonation is identified as the fundamental cause of the “skin-core” performance gradient in large bricks. Notably, the CaCO3 polymorph assemblage, crystal morphology, and spatial distribution of carbonation products, rather than total CO2 uptake alone, govern pore structure and mechanical strength, and the long-term stability of metastable polymorphs requires attention. Although a few pilot-scale studies have demonstrated technical feasibility, most research remains confined to laboratory-scale small specimens. Scaling up to industrial production still faces three major constraints: insufficient long-term volume stability evidence, lack of standards and specifications, and limited slag consumption capacity. This review focuses on shaped steel slag bricks and blocks. Where brick-scale data are limited, findings from powders, single minerals, pastes, aggregates, and aqueous carbonation are used as mechanistic support rather than direct evidence of brick performance.
