AB  - Polyanionic compounds have emerged as highly promising cathode materials for sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) because their strong covalent frameworks provide high thermal stability, long cycle life, and resource advantages. This review summarizes four representative families, namely phosphates, sulfates, silicates, and fluorophosphates or mixed polyanions, and highlights how structural units, inductive effects, and ion transport channels together determine electrochemical performance. In phosphate systems, o-NaFePO4, Na3V2(PO4)3, and Na4Fe3(PO4)2(P2O7) demonstrate the benefits of stable frameworks, multidimensional Na+ diffusion pathways, and low structural strain. Sulfates deliver high operating voltages through the strong inductive effect of SO42− but still suffer from moisture sensitivity and poor thermodynamic stability. Silicates offer ultrahigh theoretical capacities enabled by multi-electron reactions but are limited by low electronic conductivity and sluggish kinetics. Fluorophosphates and mixed polyanions combine high voltage and fast Na+ transport through the electron withdrawing effect of F− and open frameworks, although fluorine loss and structural defects remain major challenges. This review further discusses sodium storage mechanisms, including diffusion channel dimensionality, migration barriers, charge compensation, and phase transitions, and emphasizes the value of in-situ and multi scale characterization in guiding the design of high voltage, high rate, and long life polyanionic cathodes.
