AB  - Hydrogen safety remains a critical bottleneck constraining its widespread adoption. This article focuses on the engine room and establishes a coupled numerical model covering the entire hydrogen leakage, diffusion, and explosion process. And systematically studies the effect and disaster evolution process under the multiple factors such as ignition position, leakage pressure and ventilation conditions. Quantitatively analyze the explosion overpressure, temperature variation laws. Results indicate that hydrogen diffusion is dominated by buoyancy, readily forming non-uniform combustible clouds at the ceiling. The ignition location is the critical factor affecting explosion intensity. When the leakage pressure increases from 6 MPa to 12 MPa, the hydrogen diffusion area changes from 2138.5 m3 to 2530.4 m3, and the dangerous area increases from 165.0 m3 to 1714.3 m3. Increasing ventilation velocity can reduce the hydrogen cloud scale, effectively suppressing explosion overpressure, temperature, and flame propagation. At a ventilation velocity of 12 m/s, the hydrogen diffusion and hazardous area reduce to 1908.3 m3 and 135.1 m3. The overpressure damage range shrinks to 684.3 m3, while the high-temperature damage range decreases to 1385.5 m3. Some risk prevention and control strategies are proposed. The research can provide theoretical and data support for risk assessment, explosion prevention and suppression design.
