AB  - The issue of secondary contaminant sources in low-permeability zones (LPZs), resulting from aquifer heterogeneity, is considered a global challenge in the field of groundwater remediation. Conventional in situ remediation technologies (such as pump-and-treat and in situ chemical oxidation) rely heavily on advective transport. However, their inherent mass-transfer limitations make it difficult to counteract the contaminant release process dominated by molecular diffusion in LPZs. As a result, remediation often requires extended time frames, incurs high costs, and is prone to frequent contaminant rebound. In recent years, groundwater circulation wells (GCWs) technology, an innovative in situ groundwater remediation approach, has demonstrated significant potential in addressing the back diffusion problem from LPZs. This review comprehensively examines the mechanisms, applications, and research progress of GCW technology in remediating back diffusion from LPZs. It begins with a systematic analysis of the multi-scale formation mechanisms of secondary contaminant sources in LPZs, ranging from macroscopic heterogeneity to microscopic pore structure and biogeochemical processes. Furthermore, this review elaborates on two core remediation mechanisms of GCWs in addressing secondary contamination in LPZs: enhanced interfacial mass transfer and optimized reagent delivery. GCWs markedly accelerate interfacial mass flux, though deep LPZ transport remains diffusion-limited. Additionally, it systematically summarizes the key factors influencing GCWs performance. Finally, this review provides a critical assessment of the application limitations of GCWs, identifies key knowledge gaps and outlines future research directions, aiming to offer guidance for the sustainable management of complex contaminated sites.
