AB  - This study investigates fault gas generation resulting from insulation defects in oil-immersed inverted current transformers during extended operation. A novel method for analysing fault gas propagation is proposed, combining multiphysics coupling simulations with experimental validation. The approach begins with the development of a simplified model of the oil-immersed inverted current transformer, followed by the construction of an electro-thermal-flow multiphysics coupling model to describe the diffusion characteristics of fault gases during discharge. The gas diffusion process is simulated using finite element software, and an experimental platform is established to validate the results. Experimental data reveal that Hydrogen(H2) has a significantly higher diffusion coefficient compared to other gases, indicating its faster propagation within the oil-immersed medium. In the early stages of fault development, the gas primarily diffuses towards the oil surface, at a rate approximately twice that towards the bottom of the oil-immersed inverted current transformer. Over time, the gas distribution becomes more homogeneous throughout the oil-immersed inverted current transformer. This research elucidates the diffusion behaviors of fault gases in oil-immersed inverted current transformers, providing both a theoretical foundation and experimental insights for fault diagnosis and early warning systems in oil-immersed power equipment.
