AB  - Plant-microbe molecular dialogue, governing immunity, pathogenesis, and symbiosis, has been largely studied in simplified laboratory systems. This review argues that such a paradigm is fundamentally incomplete, as it ignores the decisive role of the soil matrix itself. We synthesize evidence that the physical architecture (pore networks, water dynamics, and texture) and chemical state (pH, redox potential, and mineralogy) of soil actively govern the diffusion, stability, transformation, and perception of signaling molecules. This positions soil not as a passive backdrop but as the central molecular battlefield where physicochemical properties define the rules of engagement. We detail how these non-biological factors spatially constrain microbial motility, filter chemical communication, and dynamically reshape the rhizosphere environment. By integrating perspectives from soil science, biophysics, and molecular biology, and by highlighting emerging methodologies, this framework bridges the critical gap between mechanistic discovery and field application. Ultimately, we contend that a predictive understanding of plant-microbe outcomes and the development of novel, sustainable disease management strategies requires mastering the physics and chemistry of this battlefield.
