KW  - Lateral flow assay
KW  - Mechanistic modeling
KW  - Capillary flow
KW  - Convection-diffusion-reaction
KW  - AI-Empowered mechanistic modeling
KW  - Inverse design
AB  - Lateral flow assay (LFA) performance is governed by the coupled processes of capillary flow, mass transport, and biochemical reactions. However, these internal processes are difficult to observe directly, making LFA design and optimization heavily dependent on empirical trial-and-error. Mechanistic modeling provides a means to describe these processes quantitatively and to relate internal transport and reaction behavior to assay performance. Despite these advances, there have been no LFA simulation-related reviews in the past five years to summarize the progress. This review therefore systematically examines mechanistic modeling in LFA, from capillary wicking to coupled flow-transport-reaction processes and emerging mechanistic modeling strategies empowered by artificial intelligence (AI). We first compare saturated and unsaturated flow models, including Lucas-Washburn, Darcy-based, Richards, and two-phase models, with emphasis on their assumptions, applicability, and limitations. We then summarize multiphysics convection-diffusion-reaction (CDR) models that couple flow, mass transport, and reaction kinetics to describe signal formation and support sensitivity enhancement, dynamic range expansion, and structural optimization. Building on these mechanistic foundations, we further discuss simulation data-driven machine learning, machine learning-assisted mechanistic modeling, and physics-informed machine learning as three strategies for improving prediction, optimization, and parameter inference. Alongside these advances, we discuss current challenges associated with model assumptions, parameter uncertainty, and experimental validation. By comparing these approaches within a unified model-selection framework, this review provides practical guidance for selecting models according to research objectives and dominant physical processes, and supports the transition of LFA development from empirical optimization toward predictive and mechanism-guided design.
